# loadTokenizer Shared Entry-Point Note

First note what is near the entry point. loadTokenizer is in `packages/transformers/src/tokenization_utils.js`; the imports for the tokenizer, templates, and general-purpose utilities are all visible here.

```js
import { Tokenizer } from '@huggingface/tokenizers';
import { Template } from '@huggingface/jinja';
import { Callable } from './utils/generic.js';
import { isIntegralNumber, mergeArrays } from './utils/core.js';
```

These items are in the same file. The specific usage points for `Callable` have not yet been recorded; `isIntegralNumber` and `mergeArrays` are retained as local utility dependencies for now, to be pinned down to concrete symbols when calls to loadTokenizer need to be traced.

## Checks around the entry point

Record shared imports and callers separately first, to avoid treating neighboring names as implementations at the same layer.

# Qwen Model Family Compatibility Notes

This record originally only reused the Qwen2VL processor test entry point. The entry point is at `packages/transformers/tests/models/qwen2_vl/test_processor_qwen2_vl.js`, and the objects being checked at the time were processor loading and image assets, not the entire Qwen model directory.

The old path directly takes `AutoProcessor` and `Qwen2VLProcessor` from `transformers.js`, with images provided by `load_cached_image`; loading and execution times still use `MAX_PROCESSOR_LOAD_TIME` and `MAX_TEST_EXECUTION_TIME`. This arrangement made it easy to reuse existing cases, but it incorrectly turned "Qwen can run" into "Qwen Model Family has been checked."

During migration, do not replace the existing Qwen2VL test case yet. Keep it as the processor entry point first, then record the model type, the selected processor, and the result left by a single call separately. Store failed samples separately from successful samples as well, to avoid judging solely by successful loading.

Provisional order:

1. Keep the original Qwen2VL processor test case;
2. Create a separate record for the model type to be checked;
3. Record the names, input types, and exceptions visible in successful and failed cases;
4. If the new record cannot reproduce the processor result from the old test case, return to the Qwen2VL path and do not merge the conclusions.

The selection rule for the model directory has not yet been clearly documented here.

# Qwen Model Family Compatibility Notes

The earliest checks were narrower in scope than the title suggests. This page grew out of the Qwen2VL processor test and was originally intended to provide an entry point that could be revisited for prerelease versions; later, some people treated it as evidence that compatibility checks for the model family had been completed. That use is inaccurate, but the old entry point still has value: it brings the processor, cached images, and two time limits into the same local test, making it useful for first confirming that the migrated checks have not lost any existing paths.

The retained entry point is `packages/transformers/tests/models/qwen2_vl/test_processor_qwen2_vl.js`. At the time, the top of the file showed:

```js
import { AutoProcessor, Qwen2VLProcessor } from "../../../src/transformers.js";
import { load_cached_image } from "../../asset_cache.js";
import { MAX_PROCESSOR_LOAD_TIME, MAX_TEST_EXECUTION_TIME } from "../../init.js";
```

These lines only show where the old check obtained its processor, images, and time limits. This did not make Qwen2VLProcessor the default processor for all Qwen configurations, nor did it provide the same set of inputs for every type in the model catalog. The old draft glossed over this distinction, so "the test file exists" can no longer stand in for compatibility results.

---

## Archived Old Procedure

The original procedure was straightforward: open the existing Qwen2VL test, confirm that `AutoProcessor` and `Qwen2VLProcessor` can still be used by that test, obtain the images from `load_cached_image`, and run it under the constraints associated with `MAX_PROCESSOR_LOAD_TIME` and `MAX_TEST_EXECUTION_TIME`. If everything along this path worked, write "Qwen has been checked" in the record.

That old conclusion is retained here to explain why the records were later split; it is no longer suitable as a sign-off statement. What it actually covered was a single vision processor test entry point. Model type resolution, constructor selection, and differences between text and multimodal configurations were not covered by that statement.

The procedure for restoring the old path also remains unchanged: when a newly created family-level record has not yet specified the processor selection, return to this Qwen2VL test case first, rather than inserting other model types into the original test. This restores the existing processor check rather than assuming that all candidate models should go through the same input preparation.

```text
Old: Qwen2VL test entry point
    └─ Qwen2VLProcessor + cached images

After migration: candidate model types
        ├─ Record catalog selection
        ├─ Record processor entry point
        └─ Keep separate success and failure samples
```

## What Was Actually Separated During Migration

The first step is not to rename the test, but to document "model type" and "processor test case" separately. The model type is the key used when selecting from the catalog; the processor test case is the local entry point for preparing inputs. The two sometimes appear together, but they are not interchangeable fields.

HFJ-214 later required retaining the visible results of one successful call and one failed call. The search terms used in that card are `Qwen2MoeForCausalLM` and `Qwen3NextForCausalLM`. These two names should remain in their respective model selection records and must not be backfilled into the Qwen2VL processor test to make it look as though that test has already loaded them.

The migration records should start with the candidate models, with a separate short section for each candidate:

- State the model type being checked;
- State the model constructor name requested at the call site;
- State whether a processor is needed for this call and which processor was actually selected;
- For image inputs, note whether `load_cached_image` is still being used;
- For a successful sample, retain only the type and return shape obtained from this call;
- For a failure sample, retain the original exception, the triggering input, and the location where it occurred.

The "return shape" here is only an observation field in the migration record and should not be filled in with a uniform format in advance. The image inputs from the old test may suit Qwen2VL, but they are not necessarily the inputs that other candidates should use. When there is no corresponding sample, leaving the field blank is better than copying an old result.

Processor selection is only the second step. For candidates that still need vision processing, you can start by recording the automatic selection path for `AutoProcessor`; specify `Qwen2VLProcessor` in the same record only when a fixed processor is needed to reproduce the old Qwen2VL behavior. Results should be retained separately for the two paths. If the automatically selected name differs from the fixed processor, do not immediately label the difference as a regression: first confirm whether that candidate model was supposed to require a different kind of processor.

The third step is to check which stages the time limits apply to. `MAX_PROCESSOR_LOAD_TIME` constrains the processor-loading portion; `MAX_TEST_EXECUTION_TIME` is the existing limit on the overall test execution side. The migration record should distinguish whether a timeout occurred during loading or execution. The old draft only said "timeout," making it impossible later to determine whether image preparation, processor selection, or the model call had added time.

## Switching Between the Old Path and the New Records

Switching does not mean putting all Qwen names into one parameter table. Migrate only one clearly identified candidate at a time, create a result record first, and then decide whether another candidate is needed. The reason is not batch management, but to prevent vision processor failures from masking model catalog failures.

The recommended sequence is:

1. Use the old Qwen2VL test case to confirm that the basic processor entry point is still present;
2. Select a candidate model type and record the constructor name provided by the catalog;
3. If the candidate needs image inputs, specify whether it will continue to use cached images or use another input source;
4. First record the processor and model names observed in one successful call;
5. Then record one failed call, without reducing the exception to "does not work";
6. Add the candidate to this page's migration checklist only when there is one local record each for success and failure.

Step 5 in particular must not be skipped. A successful call shows that a particular set of inputs can pass; a failed call is what preserves evidence of an unsuitable processor, missing input requirements, or a mismatch in catalog selection. If the two are mixed into one paragraph, later reviewers can only tell that "it was tried before," without knowing which entry point to use to reproduce it.

Callers still using the old test do not need to migrate away immediately just because candidates have been added to the model catalog. As long as they continue to use Qwen2VLProcessor, they should cover only Qwen2VL processor regression checks. Callers that need family-level conclusions should instead read the separate model records; when a record lacks complete success and failure samples, retain the old call for now and do not merge blank entries into the existing test.

## Restoring the Old Path

If any of the following occurs during migration, restoration should be limited to the current candidate rather than rolling back the entire Qwen check:

- The automatically selected processor does not match the expected inputs;
- Image preparation does not match the candidate model's input contract;
- The loading phase hits `MAX_PROCESSOR_LOAD_TIME`, but the execution phase has not started;
- The execution phase hits `MAX_TEST_EXECUTION_TIME`, and processor operations and model calls have not yet been separated;
- The failure sample contains only a one-sentence overview, with no way to identify the actual exception or input.

The restoration action is to withdraw the newly added records for that candidate, rerun the original Qwen2VL test entry point, and mark the candidate as incomplete. Do not delete existing failure fragments; they only show that this migration has not yet produced a stable check, not that the model type has been ruled out.

The original draft recommended "always fall back to Qwen2VLProcessor when a failure occurs." That recommendation no longer applies. Failures may occur during model type resolution, processor selection, image preparation, or execution, and uniformly replacing the processor with a fixed one would hide these differences. A fixed processor is now used only when reproducing the old Qwen2VL test case; other candidates retain their own selection records.

## Card Links Added Later

By the time HFJ-214 was followed up further, the original owner had already moved on to handling a production incident, and someone else took over the card. This handoff did not add any new conclusions about models, but it explains why this page retains both the early narrow-scope records and the later sample requirements. The card is used to locate the success and failure results that still need to be added; the page itself still preserves the old migration procedure and the boundaries for restoring the old path.

DOC-27 discusses the validation design for Range Transport, which is not in the same migration batch as the processor entry point here. The two pages can link to each other, but do not write transport probe results into the Qwen Model Family records, and do not use this page's processor test as a substitute for the validation there.

## What Is Still Missing

- The old entry point does not list which candidate models actually use image inputs;
- Success and failure samples for `Qwen2MoeForCausalLM` and `Qwen3NextForCausalLM` need to be archived separately;
- A reproducible comparison is still missing for cases where model catalog selection and automatic processor selection disagree;
- It is still unconfirmed whether the time limits from the old Qwen2VL test case can be reused directly for family-level checks.

This page preserves the approach used at the time to migrate away from a single processor test. It is useful for looking back at why the old check was split; when integrating new models, their actual samples should still be added first, rather than treating the steps here as a support table for the current catalog.
